WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.173
One lung cancer study
reports two response rates.

00:00:04.353 --> 00:00:07.350
Which one belongs in a
competitive intelligence brief?

00:00:08.297 --> 00:00:11.805
In Aperture, an analyst can
start with the number itself.

00:00:11.985 --> 00:00:14.373
Select a result in the benchmark table,

00:00:14.373 --> 00:00:17.202
then switch the AI copilot to Ask.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.652
The selected cell travels
with the question.

00:00:20.832 --> 00:00:24.149
Here, the analyst asks
which figure to lead with,

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:26.152
who it covers, and where it came from.

00:00:27.088 --> 00:00:29.534
The copilot finds both groups.

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:32.997
Across all 137 patients at this dose,

00:00:32.997 --> 00:00:36.582
the confirmed response
rate was 48.2 percent.

00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:42.325
The 56.3 percent figure comes from the
32 patients treated in second line.

00:00:42.325 --> 00:00:45.397
Those patients are already
included in the larger group.

00:00:46.338 --> 00:00:50.532
Before using the answer, the analyst
opens the underlying readout.

00:00:50.712 --> 00:00:54.357
The saved passage puts the
rate beside its denominator,

00:00:54.357 --> 00:00:55.906
with a link back to the study.

00:00:56.838 --> 00:01:00.726
A follow-up in Edit mode changes
the comparison table itself.

00:01:00.906 --> 00:01:03.636
The analyst asks for four measures.

00:01:03.636 --> 00:01:08.182
Aperture applies the change and
records it, with an option to undo.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.751
The analyst saves the answer as a brief,

00:01:11.751 --> 00:01:15.241
checks the wording, and keeps
the two populations explicit.

00:01:15.421 --> 00:01:18.939
Once reviewed, the brief can
travel with the shared dashboard.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.821
The next reader gets the number,

00:01:21.821 --> 00:01:24.843
the patients it describes,
and the source to check.
